---
import ManualLayout from '../../layouts/ManualLayout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/paths';
---

<ManualLayout slug="getting-started">
  <h2 id="opening">Opening your first collection</h2>
  <p>
    HelloNotes works on a <strong>collection</strong>: an ordinary folder of Markdown files. There
    is no import, no library format and no database — you simply point the app at a folder.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first time you launch it there is already one open: the collection that ships inside the
    app, a short tour and the user manual, written in HelloNotes. It is a folder of notes like any
    other, so you can edit it, close it, and bring it back later with{' '}
    <strong>Open Default Collection</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>To open a folder of your own:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Click the <strong>+</strong> at the top of the sidebar and choose{' '}
      <strong>Open Collection ▸ from Folder…</strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      Pick any folder containing <code>.md</code> files — or an empty folder to start something new.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>File ▸ Open…</strong> (<kbd>⌘O</kbd>) does the same from one screen that also lists the
    collections you have opened recently. An existing <strong>Obsidian vault</strong> works as-is:
    choose <strong>Open Collection ▸ from Obsidian Vault…</strong> or pick the vault’s folder, and
    it opens directly, wiki-links, tags, aliases and front matter intact.
  </p>

  <h2 id="layout">The layout</h2>
  <p>
    The window is three regions, each about one thing: the collection on the left, the note in the
    middle, and what the note is on the right.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The sidebar</strong> is the collection. At its top are the collection’s tools as
      named rows — <strong>Mind Map</strong>, <strong>Assistant</strong> and{' '}
      <strong>Git</strong> (a collection connected straight to a cloud account has no Git row) —
      and below them a single tree:{' '}
      <strong>Recents</strong> and <strong>Bookmarks</strong> pinned first (each spans every open
      collection, which is why neither is a folder), then one row per open collection, with its
      folders and notes beneath it exactly as they are on disk. The <strong>+</strong> in its
      header makes a folder and opens or creates collections. The header also holds the
      sidebar’s own toggle; while the sidebar is hidden, that toggle is the first button of the
      bar over the note, so the sidebar can always come back.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The note</strong> is in the middle. The bar over it has three buttons —{' '}
      <strong>+</strong> for a new note, a magnifying glass for searching and finding, and a gear
      for Settings — then the tabs of the notes you have open, then{' '}
      <strong>Note Actions</strong>, the menu of the open note’s own commands. Along the
      bottom are the four views: Edit, Preview, Markdown and Split.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The right panel</strong> is what the open note is: its{' '}
      <strong>Summary &amp; Outline</strong>, <strong>Tags</strong>, <strong>Links</strong>,{' '}
      <strong>Properties</strong>, <strong>History</strong> and <strong>Graph</strong>. The last
      button in the bar shows or hides it, and the row of icons along its top chooses what it
      shows. The note beside it stays live, so you can keep typing with the panel open.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The Mind Map and the Assistant are about the whole collection rather than one note, so they
    open as <strong>tabs</strong> beside your notes. Choosing a note puts them behind
    it; their tab brings them back. HelloNotes opens no window of notes on its own — a second one
    appears only when you ask for it, with <strong>New Window</strong> or{' '}
    <strong>Open in New Window</strong>. (Settings, on the Mac, is the usual settings window.)
  </p>
  <p>
    Several collections can be open at once, and a pinned place that is empty is left out rather
    than shown empty. Searching is deliberately <em>library-wide</em>:{' '}
    <strong>Search Notes</strong>, under the magnifying glass (or <kbd>⌥⌘F</kbd>), opens a search
    field in the bar, looks across every open collection, and shows the results in the sidebar,
    grouped by collection.
  </p>
  <p>
    The layout follows the space available, not the device. In a window taller than it is wide — an
    iPad in portrait — the sidebar lies across the top instead, folders on the left and the notes
    in the chosen folder on the right. In a narrow window, and on an iPhone, there is room for one
    thing at a time: four tabs along the bottom — <strong>Notes</strong>, <strong>Search</strong>,{' '}
    <strong>Tags</strong> and <strong>AI</strong> — with the open note in a strip above them that
    brings it up full screen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first-note">Creating your first note</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Press <kbd>⌘N</kbd>, or choose <strong>New Note</strong> from the <strong>+</strong> in the
      bar.
    </li>
    <li>
      A file is created where you are — in the folder of the note you have selected, or at the top
      of the collection — and opened for editing.
    </li>
    <li>
      Type as you would in any Markdown editor. Formatting appears as you type — see{' '}
      <a href={href('/manual/editor')}>The editor</a>.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A note’s title <em>is</em> its filename. It is shown above the body as a heading you can edit in
    place: click it, type, and press <kbd>Return</kbd> to go back to the text. Renaming there renames
    the file and rewrites every link to that note elsewhere in the collection — as does{' '}
    <strong>Note ▸ Rename…</strong> (<kbd>⇧⌘R</kbd>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="saving">Saving</h2>
  <p>
    There is no save command, because there is nothing to remember to do. A note is written to disk
    when you stop editing it — when you leave the text, switch to another note or another app,
    close the window or quit. A save that works is never announced; the bottom bar says{' '}
    <strong>Save failed</strong> only in the one case that matters, when your words are not on
    disk.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a note changes on disk while you are editing it — a sync client, another app, another device
    — HelloNotes tells you rather than silently picking a winner, and offers{' '}
    <strong>Reload</strong> or <strong>Keep Mine</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next">Where to go next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={href('/manual/editor')}>The editor</a> — live Markdown, view modes, maths and diagrams.</li>
    <li><a href={href('/manual/links-and-graph')}>Links &amp; the graph</a> — connect notes together.</li>
    <li><a href={href('/manual/organising')}>Organising notes</a> — search, tags, daily notes and templates.</li>
  </ul>
</ManualLayout>
